From 478102d79e3a0b25bc7a78d92180a769c7b4a3dd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:32:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: ask which edition of a Matroska file to play,
 and play an ordered one with cuts

A Matroska file can hold several editions (a theatrical and an extended
cut); ffmpeg-9940 exports them. With the new setting "Ask which edition
when a file has several" (videoplayer.hadesmkveditions, Player > Videos,
off by default) and two or more editions that can be played, Kodi asks
which one when the file starts.

- The question is asked on the application thread before the player
  opens the file (CApplication::PlayFile), never from the player's
  thread, where a modal question could leave Kodi waiting on itself if
  playback were stopped meanwhile. Only the header of the file is read
  (custom I/O over CFile, no stream probing, 10 s deadline). Only a
  confirmed choice counts; anything else plays the file as it is stored.
  A restart of the player does not ask again.
- The choice goes to the player as two item properties (index and
  EditionUID), which VideoPlayer checks against what its own demuxer
  reads. An edition that is not ordered plays the whole file; an ordered
  one plays as the whole file with the parts it leaves out added as cuts
  to the edit list (CEdl::AddCuts(), new; each cut through AddEdit(),
  which refuses one that overlaps an edit already there), after the edit
  decision list files are read, at every opening of the video stream.
- The demuxer says in kodi.log, without debug logging, which editions a
  file has, which can be offered and why the others cannot (hidden,
  segment linking, parts out of order, incomplete); an ordinary file with
  one edition that is not ordered says nothing.

With the setting off nothing changes but those log lines. Limits: the
chapter list stays the demuxer's, not the chosen edition's; each cut is
a seek, not seamless.

Strings #39805-#39808. The rules are in MatroskaEditions.h, whose host
test now also runs Chosen() and Describe() (77 checks; 15 mutations make
it fail).
